Myth: In a harbor town where boats slept on their reflections, there lived a clockmaker named Kestrel who kept time not with gears but with breaths. Each dawn, the harbor exhaled a pale mist, and Kestrel listened, mapping every whisper of the water to a watch’s rhythm. People came with minutes that trembled—an hour wasted to worry, a second stolen by fear—and Kestrel would wind them back, not by force, but by companionship.

One dusk, a shy gull with a chipped wing landed upon his bench, dropping a seed the color of a shadow just before sunset. “Plant this,” the gull seemed to say, “and time will bend toward what your heart longs most.” Kestrel buried the seed in a pocket of the quay where the tide keeps secrets. Night after night, the seed sprouted into a tree of clocks, each leaf a different moment: the moment of a first greeting, the moment a storm eased, the moment a coin found its mate.

When the tree sang, people remembered what they truly cherished—the courage to start anew, the patient listening that mends quarrels, the shared breath of community. Time ceased to lose itself; it gathered, like boats returning to harbor, toward the loves we keep in one another.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

